NAME
       dig - ask DNS servers about names and addresses

SYNOPSIS
       dig [@SERVER] NAME [TYPE] [+short]
       dig -x ADDRESS

DESCRIPTION
       dig sends a DNS question to the servers in /etc/resolv.conf (or to
       @SERVER) and prints the full answer: the header with the status
       (NOERROR, or NXDOMAIN when the name does not exist), the question, the
       ANSWER SECTION with the records, and which server answered and how
       fast.

       Unlike getent hosts, dig asks DNS only and ignores /etc/hosts, so it
       tells you whether the problem is in DNS or elsewhere.

       dig is in the bind-utils package, together with host and nslookup.

OPTIONS
       @SERVER
              Ask this server instead of the ones in /etc/resolv.conf.

       TYPE   The kind of record: A (IPv4 address, the default), AAAA
              (IPv6), MX (mail servers), NS, CNAME, PTR, ANY.

       -x ADDRESS
              A reverse lookup: which name has this address?

       +short Print only the answer itself.

EXAMPLES
       Look up an address:

           dig server2.lab.example.com

       Only the address, for a script:

           dig +short utility.lab.example.com

       Ask the lab's DNS server directly:

           dig @192.168.50.254 repo.lab.example.com

SEE ALSO
       host(1), nslookup(1), getent(1), resolv.conf(5)
